[
 {:seon.task/name "the-helper-stays-while-agents-work"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: the owner rules whether the embedding model stays up while any agent is live (it changes the 2026-10-29 ruling that it spins down when idle), and what he is shown is the measure below, so measure first. For the owner's review: it changes the ruling that the embedding model spins down when idle, docs/decisions.md (2026-10-29, \"if it's cheap\"). The helper ends when its queue empties (seon.vector/close), so every :embed starts a process and loads the model: 950 to 1,000 ms measured 2026-11-06 at load 5, against 0.45 s when the ruling was made and 5 to 15 ms for one text with the model up. A turn that writes a form pays it (seon.agent/embed-written!, on the agent's thread). Option: root keeps the helper up while any agent is live and closes it when none is, its workload rather than a clock. Cost: about 0.86 GB RSS held for as long as agents run, against the 16 GB target (:seon.config.machine/memory-mb, bin/check scale). Measure first how many turns of a real run still embed now eval code is no text: if few, leave it. Proofs: a second :embed with an agent live starts no process."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-commit-of-no-text-starts-no-round"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by Optimization (2026-11-10, building main's content mark, docs/storage.md#mains-content-mark). seon.vector/vectors-proc runs a round at every main commit notice (seon.vector/round: the commit's db made, one datoms-diff since the last round's commit, due's seeks), though nearly every commit is records (an agent's status per request) or its own embedding write, which hold no text. The notice already carries the commit's resolved datoms (:ops), as the test service and the fork server read them (seon.program/moves-program?). Cut: a notice whose :ops hold no datom of a text attribute (seon.vector/texts), with nothing owed and the proc caught up, starts no round; the next round's diff from the last commit it scanned still finds every text since. The proc's tests build notices without :ops (vector-test's note): they must carry the commit's ops, as the writer's do. Estimate first: what a round costs on a records-only commit on a 2,931-form main (n = the datoms since the last round; expected the diff and an at-commit, under 1 ms), and how often it runs (every request). Proof: seon.vector-test/a-commit-of-no-text-starts-no-round (a status-only notice: no round, no read; a form's notice: a round)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.vector-test/a-commit-of-no-text-starts-no-round}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-cold-near-costs-its-shortlist-not-its-leaves"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by context (2026-11-15 ~09:05, landing 4's estimate, root's REPL on main). [M] seon.vector/near over the forms (:of :form/id, 6,354 rows, shortlist 400) cold: 47M ticks / 327 MB; the same call again at once: 5.2M / 31 MB; once 170M / 1.8 GB (result/a0b2ca5d, result/ccd89b79). The rows are kept (rows-of: 119 ticks a hit) and fine-scores over 400 warm costs 4.1M / 20 MB, so the gap is the cold reads of the shortlist's :seon.vector/f16 values (1,536 bytes each, over :seon.config.store/value-out-of-line-bytes), each seek loading and decoding a leaf node (H: zstd node + out-of-line fetch per value). Lower bound: 400 x 1.5 KB = 0.6 MB read; 10x over is ~6 MB. Find where the bytes go (seon.profile/measure! with the store's node cache cold), then fix at the storage cause (keep the f16 rows beside the coarse rows as rows-of keeps those, or read them in entity order so one leaf serves many). seon.ctx.search/relevance pays this every cold call (119M-190M ticks whole)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/estimate {seon.vector/near {:n 400 :order :linear :ticks 6000000 :bytes 6000000}}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.vector-cost-test/a-cold-near-costs-its-shortlist}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-boot-with-nothing-changed-scans-no-text"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: context; runtime reviews (root filed it 2026-11-15 from runtime's measure, agent m14d8 result/b86d98a8). PRIOR WORK: seon.vector/round (vector.clj:538) is O(d) after its first round: it keeps {:clean :seen :owed} in the proc's state and diffs from :seen (seon.vector/written, d/datoms-diff); the first round (clean nil) and a failure fall back to seon.vector/due over every text. Task a-commit-of-no-text-starts-no-round (the notice's :ops) is the sibling for a records-only commit. CAUSE: what the proc learned lives only in proc state, so every boot forgets it and scans all 10,392 texts: 23-31M ticks, 180 MB, 0 due, holding root's stop ~170 ms [M runtime]. DESIGN, data first: the round's result is a fact on main, written in the same tx as the vectors it writes (one datom more, no extra commit): {:commit :owed} on one entity (names settled with data first); the first round reads it in place of nil. A boot at the marker's commit: nothing due, one seek. Main moved since: seon.vector/written from the marker's commit plus a seek per owed. A swept marker commit is kept by seon.db/retain!'s kept references, or filed with data, never guarded here. MEASURED [M root 2026-11-15]: from the last vector write (t 536896435) to main (95 tx later): diff + due 6.1M ticks / 18.8 MB, 0 written, 0 due (result/aeaa640a); finding that commit by walking the branch (seon.ctx.wire/at-t) 5.7M more (result/b8a7e969), which the marker's commit removes. COST: nothing moved: 1 seek, ~5k ticks, ~10 KB (the lower bound, O(1)); d text datoms moved: the diff of what differs; the write +1 datom a round that writes, ~2k ticks. DELETES: the boot's full scan; due's 2-arity once a failure also resumes from the marker (a failed write commits nothing, so the marker stays at the last good round). Proof, red first: seon.vector-test/a-boot-with-nothing-changed-scans-no-text (a proc started on a main whose marker names its commit asks nothing and reads no text datom: counted reads, not time)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.vector-test/a-boot-with-nothing-changed-scans-no-text}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.vector{round {:n 95, :order :linear, :ticks 6000000, :bytes 20000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-texts-vector-write-costs-kilobytes"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: context (root, 2026-11-16). Root's measure: on the clean store the vectors proc's backfill did 5.9B ticks and allocated 18.6 GB in the writer process for 3,648 texts, 1.6M ticks and 5 MB a text, the model's native work aside; 23.5B ticks after a plain restart of the old 15.8 GB store. BOUND (docs/cost.md#what-things-cost-here): about 60k ticks and 60 KB a text (its 8 datoms one small transaction, its pack 12k ticks, seeks and the frame's decode a few k). PRIOR WORK: seon.vector/round and vectors-proc, seon.ctx.search/embedded-tx, seon.vector/pack, the :embed effect (seon.vector/embed, sock/frame by konserve.jolt.fasl); siblings a-commit-of-no-text-starts-no-round and a-boot-with-nothing-changed-scans-no-text. MEASURED (root's REPL on the clean standing store, 10,382 texts, 2026-11-16): a full due scan 44.5M ticks, 332 MB [result/ce3efc35]; a steady round of 64 owed texts 1.5-2.2M ticks, 4.6-9 MB [result/cd0ebaea]; the :embed answer's decode 0.17M, 3.6 MB [fasl, 64x768 floats]; 64 packs 0.75M, 3.9 MB; tx-data 0.2M, embedded-tx 1.4M, the write applied (seon.db/with) 5.9M ticks and 12 MB, 513 datoms [result/c61813a9]. Sum about 9M ticks and 25 MB a round, 140k ticks and 0.4 MB a text: about 10% of root's figure. NEXT: place the other 90% by reading root's counter (which process, which reading) and one round's writer-process work beside it: a full due scan each round (its bytes are near the measured per-round 330 MB) or the work every commit notice wakes in the writer process (the reconciler, the render proc). Then fix at the cause and hold the row."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.vector-test/a-texts-vector-write-costs-kilobytes}
  :seon.task/estimate {seon.vector/round {:n 64, :order :linear, :ticks 4000000, :bytes 4000000}}}
